   Following [1], the first step is to replace Forrelation by a “continuous relaxation” of the
problem: a version that is strictly easier (and thus, for which proving a lower bound is harder ),
but which has rotational symmetry that will be extremely convenient for us. Thus, in Real
Forrelation, we are given oracle access to two real functions f, g : {0, 1}n → R. As usual, the
“input size” is N = 2n . We are promised that the pair hf, gi was drawn from one of two probability
measures:

    (i) In the uniform measure U , every f (x) and g (y) value is an independent N (0, 1) Gaussian.

 (ii) In the forrelated measure F, every f (x) value is an independent N (0, 1) Gaussian, while
      every g (y) value is fixed to
