We now proceed to a lower bound on the query complexity of Real Forrelation. As a first
step, let us recast our problem more abstractly. For convenience, we will use ket notation (|vi,
|wi, etc.) for vectors in RN , even if the vectors do not represent quantum states and are not even
normalized. Let |1i , . . . , |N i be an orthonormal basis for RN , and let |ı̂i = H |ii be the Hadamard
transform of |ii (so that |1̂i, . . . , |N̂ i is also an orthonormal basis).
    Then consider the following generalization of Real Forrelation, which we call Gaussian
Distinguishing. We are ngiven a finite set V of unitovectors in RN , called “test vectors.” (In our
case, V happens to equal |1i , . . . , |N i , |1̂i, . . . , |N̂ i .) In each step, we are allowed to pick any
test vector |vi ∈ V that hasn’t been picked in previous steps. We then “query” |vi, getting back a
real-valued response av ∈ R. The problem is to distinguish the following two cases, with constant
bias:
