10     Appendix: Property Testing
In this appendix, we show that Forrelation can be recast as a property-testing problem: that is,
as a problem of deciding whether the functions f, g : {0, 1}n → {−1, 1} satisfy a certain property,
or are far in Hamming distance from any functions satisfying that property. (For a recent survey
of quantum property-testing, see Montanaro and de Wolf [17].)
    In particular, we will obtain a property of N -bit strings that

 (1) can be quantumly ε-tested (with bounded error) using only O (1/ε) queries, but
                   √
 (2) requires Ω( logNN ) queries to ε-test classically, provided ε is a sufficiently small constant.

     This is the largest quantum versus classical property-testing separation yet known.
     Since our analysis works for k-fold Forrelation just as easily as for 2-fold, we will use the
more general setting. Let Y be the set of all k-tuples of Boolean functions hf1 , . . . , fk i such
                        1
that Φf1 ,...,fk ≤ 100    . Also, let Nε be the set of all k-tuples hg1 , . . . , gk i that differ from every
hf1 , . . . , fk i ∈ Y on at least ε · k2n of the k2n positions. Then we will be interested in the problem
of deciding whether hf1 , . . . , fk i ∈ Y or hf1 , . . . , fk i ∈ Nε , promised that one of these is the case.

                                                      56
    Our goal is to show that both our quantum algorithm for Forrelation, and our randomized
lower bound for it, carry over to the property-testing variant. There are two difficulties here. First,
one could imagine an hf1 , . . . , fk i that was far from Y in the Hamming distance sense, yet had an
                                                1
Φf1 ,...,fk value only slightly greater than 100   —in which case, we would need many repetitions of
our quantum algorithm to separate hf1 , . . . , fk i from Y. Second, one could imagine an hf1 , . . . , fk i
that was close to Y in Hamming distance, yet had (say) Φf1 ,...,fk ≥ 35 —in which case, the known
                                                        1
classical hardness of distinguishing |Φf1 ,...,fk | ≤ 100  from Φf1 ,...,fk ≥ 35 might not imply anything
about the hardness of distinguishing hf1 , . . . , fk i ∈ Y from hf1 , . . . , fk i ∈ Nε , causing the classical
lower bound to fail.18
